>>
endobj
287 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (After preparing the files, we printed the wings and evaluated the physical results. We examined the components to determine whether they had the required shape, whether their structure appeared sufficiently resistant, and whether they could withstand normal handling and assembly. We also considered their durability because the parts needed to remain in good condition throughout the preparation and testing of the car.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
288 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (In addition to the wings, we also used 3D printing to produce our own halos for the final car. Although some components were initially provided to us, preparing and printing our own halos allowed us to incorporate these pieces into the final design. Overall, the CAM stage helped us turn our digital designs into physical parts and evaluate their quality, resistance, and suitability for the completed racing car.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
289 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (The first printed versions helped us understand how our digital designs translated into physical components. By examining the printed pieces, we could identify areas that needed adjustments and determine whether the designs met our expectations. This connection between digital preparation and physical evaluation allowed us to improve the components before settling on their final versions.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
290 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (Creality Print allowed us to prepare the digital files and organize the printing process before producing the physical pieces. During this stage, we reviewed the positioning of the components and their preparation for printing to ensure that the files were suitable for the process. This step was important because the quality of the printed parts depended not only on the original design but also on how the components were prepared for production.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [14]
>>
